<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amr ibn al-As</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 573–<a href="/years/664">664 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amr ibn al-As was an Arab commander and companion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>. He led the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> in the 640s and served as its first Muslim governor, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a>. He later supported Mu'awiya, the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as.jpg" alt="Amr ibn al-As" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sherief1969 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amr_Ibn_Al_As_Mosque_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 642 a raiding force sent by <b>Amr ibn al-As</b>, probably led by Uqba ibn Nafi, met fierce resistance, and Arab writers remembered the Nubians as rumat al-hadaq, the pupil-smiters, because their archers aimed at the eyes of their enemies and blinded many.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…perhaps the most gifted battlefield commander of the seventh century, defeated a large Byzantine army over six days, in tradition aided by a dust storm, delivered Syria permanently to Arab control; Jerusalem surrendered soon afterwards, and Egypt was conquered by <b>Amr ibn al-As</b> between 640 and 642 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In late 639 <b>Amr ibn al-As</b> invaded for the Rashidun Caliphate, defeated the Roman army at Heliopolis in 640, and took the fortress of Babylon in 641.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 642 to 643 Arab forces under <b>Amr ibn al-As</b> took Barca and the Pentapolis for the Rashidun Caliphate, and the region became an Islamic province known as Barqa.</blockquote><a href="/state/roman-cyrenaica">Roman Cyrenaica</a></li><li><blockquote>After wars with the Laguatan, Arab forces under <b>Amr ibn al-As</b> took Tripoli in 643 for the Rashidun Caliphate.</blockquote><a href="/state/tripolitania">Tripolitania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">El-Fustat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=632" aria-label="Mecca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5620 1372 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Amr ibn al-As"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6070 1642h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6070 1642h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=632">What was here in 632 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
